# TODO

Updated: {{DATE}}

Use this file for lightweight reminders, future ideas, small planning points,
and inspiration that should not become a project or full work item yet.

## Rules

- `TODO`: list recent open items, newest first, numbered `1.`, `2.`, `3.` so
  {{USER_NAME}} can reply with a number to select an item. Reply in the
  preferred language while preserving IDs, file paths, commands, and project
  names.
- A plain number after a `TODO` list means select that numbered open item from
  the current `Open` ordering.
- `TODO` output must stay compact but actionable. Use a stable two-line item
  format: line 1 is only `N. TODO id / name`; line 2 is the readable
  description with the concrete next action plus any evidence, source path,
  recovery entry, or completion gate already present in the stored entry. Do
  not insert an extra blank line between one item's description and the next
  item's name. Do not mix single-line, paragraph, and blank-line-heavy styles.
  Do not reduce TODO output to bare ids, titles, or unexplained project names
  unless {{USER_NAME}} explicitly asks for a titles-only list.
- If a stored TODO is too vague to explain from `TODO.md` alone, mark it as
  `needs expansion` and offer one concrete expansion path; do not invent
  missing details or scan project repos on the micro path just to fill gaps.
- `TODO:<content>` or `TODO：<content>`: add a new open item with today's date.
  A new TODO should carry enough context for future recovery: topic/id, why it
  matters, next action, and known evidence or source path when available. If
  the prompt is only a vague reminder, store that it needs expansion or ask one
  clarifying question when immediate action is likely.
- Keep entries short. Promote an item to a work item only when it becomes a
  concrete task with files, validation, or handoff requirements.
- Do not store credentials, customer secrets, or private technical details.

